/**
 * `result<T, E>` - a value, or the reason there is not one.
 *
 * `T?` is the cheap answer when absence is enough. This is the other one: a value of type `T`, or a
 * failure of type `E`. Nothing in the compiler knows what a result is. There is no `#[core:]` binding
 * and there is deliberately none. `guard` reaches it through the two interfaces below, and
 * `.ok(...)` / `.error(...)` are the ordinary static shorthand every enum's cases get.
 *
 * Why an enum and not a struct with a flag: a struct's copy and teardown walk every property, so an
 * `ok` would still retain and release the failure it is not holding. An enum's copy and teardown are
 * per case. Same-type arms (`result<string, string>`) would also collide as two constructors of one
 * signature; cases are told apart by name.
 *
 * The layout is `T` and `E` wide, not the larger of the two. `unwrap()` hands back a `T&` into this
 * value, so the borrow lives exactly as long as the result does. See docs/stdlib/result.md.
 */

/**
 * A value of type `T`, or a failure of type `E`.
 *
 * Built with `ok` / `error`, or `.ok` / `.error` wherever the destination already names the type.
 * Read with `guard`. The methods below are the same thing spelled by hand. `match` reads it as the
 * ordinary enum it is.
 */
public enum result<T, E> : contract::unwrappable<T>, contract::failable<E>
{
    /**
     * The value arm. `E` is named nowhere in this case's signature and is bound from the owner the call
     * site wrote - `AST::static_owner_bindings` seeds it the way a receiver would for a method.
     */
    case ok(T $value);

    /**
     * The failure arm.
     *
     * `error` rather than `failure`, which is the *reader* below. One word builds and one word asks, so a
     * call site reads `return .error($e);` as the sentence it is.
     */
    case error(E $failure);

    /**
     * Is there a value? `contract::unwrappable<T>`'s first half, and what `guard` asks before anything
     * else.
     */
    const function has_value() : bool
    {
        return match ($this) {
            result<T, E>::ok($v)    => true,
            result<T, E>::error($e) => false,
        };
    }

    /**
     * True when this holds a failure - `has_value()`'s complement and nothing more.
     */
    const function is_error() : bool
    {
        return match ($this) {
            result<T, E>::ok($v)    => false,
            result<T, E>::error($e) => true,
        };
    }

    /**
     * The value. `contract::unwrappable<T>`'s other half - **only valid when there is one**, which is
     * what the protocol promises and what lets this skip a test `guard` has already performed.
     *
     * The `error` arm stops the program, and it is the reason this function shapes the way it does: that
     * arm holds an `E` and has no `T` anywhere to hand back, so stopping is the only thing it can do. A
     * `match` arm that never comes back contributes no type to the arms' unification, which is what makes
     * one arm handing back a `T&` and the other handing back nothing a legal pair.
     *
     * **Not `const`, and with no const overload.** The protocol says so: `has_value()` is const and this
     * is not, because asking *whether* promises nothing about the storage while taking the value out hands
     * back a borrow that may be written. `value_or` below is the const read, and a
     * `contract::const_unwrappable<const V>` is where a const borrow belongs the day it exists.
     */
    function unwrap() : T&
    {
        return match ($this) {
            result<T, E>::ok($v)    => $v,
            result<T, E>::error($e) => die('unwrap() on a result holding a failure'),
        };
    }

    /**
     * Why there is no value - `contract::failable<E>` in full, and what `else ($e)` binds a borrow of.
     *
     * **Only valid when there is a failure**, mirroring `unwrap()` exactly, and stopping on the other arm
     * for the same reason.
     */
    function failure() : E&
    {
        return match ($this) {
            result<T, E>::ok($v)    => die('failure() on a result holding a value'),
            result<T, E>::error($e) => $e,
        };
    }

    /**
     * The value if there is one, `$fallback` otherwise - the const read `unwrap()` cannot be.
     *
     * A **copy** of the payload rather than a borrow, which is what makes it answerable from a
     * `const result<T, E>&` at all. So this is the shape for a reader that only wants to look, and `guard`
     * stays the shape for everything else.
     */
    const function value_or(T $fallback) : T
    {
        return match ($this) {
            result<T, E>::ok($v)    => $v,
            result<T, E>::error($e) => $fallback,
        };
    }
}
